Scenario
Symptom: You power on your RHEL system and see:
GRUB rescue>
This means GRUB cannot find its configuration or boot files.
🔹 Step 1: Boot from live media
Get a RHEL installation ISO or any Linux live CD/USB.
Boot your computer from this media.
Choose “Try / Rescue” mode (doesn’t install, just runs a live system).
🔹 Step 2: Identify your system partitions
Once in the live environment, open a terminal.
Run:
lsblk
This lists all disks and partitions.
Find the root partition (where / is installed), e.g., /dev/sda2.
Find the boot partition (if separate), e.g., /dev/sda1.
You can also check:
fdisk -l
🔹 Step 3: Mount your system partitions
# Replace sda2 with your root partition
mount /dev/sda2 /mnt
If you have a separate boot partition:
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/boot
🔹 Step 4: Change root into your system
chroot /mnt
Now the terminal acts as if you’re inside your installed system.
Any GRUB commands you run now affect your real RHEL installation.
🔹 Step 5: Reinstall GRUB
For BIOS systems (legacy boot):
grub2-install /dev/sda
For UEFI systems:
grub2-install --target=x86_64-efi --efi-directory=/boot/efi --bootloader-id=redhat --recheck
/dev/sda = the disk (not partition) where bootloader goes.
🔹 Step 6: Rebuild GRUB configuration
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg   # BIOS
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg  # UEFI
This generates a new GRUB menu based on your installed kernels.
🔹 Step 7: Exit chroot and reboot
exit
umount /mnt/boot
umount /mnt
reboot
Remove the live media
Your system should now boot normally
🔹 Step 8: Check boot
If it works → success!
If still not → check:
Disk UUIDs in /etc/fstab
Kernel files in /boot
Correct BIOS/UEFI boot mode
✅ Tip for beginners:
Always keep at least one older kernel and backup GRUB config:
cp /boot/grub2/grub.cfg /boot/grub2/grub.cfg.bak

How to boot into an old kernel on RHEL if, for example, your new kernel or its initramfs is broken. I’ll explain why each step matters and its effect.

Step 1: Reboot your system
sudo reboot
Reason: You need to restart to access the GRUB menu where you can choose a different kernel.
Effect: System shuts down all running processes and starts the bootloader (GRUB).

Step 2: Access the GRUB menu
On BIOS systems: press and hold Shift during boot.
On UEFI systems: press Esc repeatedly just after the BIOS/UEFI screen.
Reason: By default, GRUB may auto-boot the default kernel, so you must interrupt it to choose another.
Effect: You see the GRUB boot menu listing all installed kernels and recovery options.

Step 3: Identify older kernels
In the GRUB menu, you’ll see something like:
Red Hat Enterprise Linux (4.18.0-505.el8.x86_64)
Red Hat Enterprise Linux (4.18.0-425.el8.x86_64)
Red Hat Enterprise Linux (4.18.0-240.el8.x86_64)
The top entry is usually the latest kernel (possibly broken). Older kernels are listed below.
Reason: You need a kernel with a working initramfs to boot successfully.
Effect: You know which kernel is safe to boot.

Step 4: Select the old kernel
Use arrow keys to highlight the old kernel you want. Press Enter.
Reason: Selecting an older kernel tells GRUB to load its vmlinuz (kernel image) and its associated initramfs instead of the default one.
Effect: System loads the chosen kernel along with its initramfs, bypassing the broken one.

Step 5: Boot into the old kernel
System will continue booting normally using the old kernel.
Reason: You need a running system to repair the new kernel or initramfs.
Effect: You now have a working Linux environment even if the new kernel is unusable.

Step 6: Verify which kernel is running
uname -r
Example output: 4.18.0-425.el8.x86_64
Reason: Confirm you are running the older kernel.
Effect: Now you can safely regenerate initramfs for the broken kernel or remove it if needed.

Step 7: Repair the broken kernel (Optional)
Once booted: sudo dracut -f /boot/initramfs-4.18.0-505.el8.x86_64.img 4.18.0-505.el8.x86_64
Reason: Recreate the missing initramfs so the new kernel can boot again.

Step 1: Boot into Rescue/Live CD
Boot from a RHEL installation DVD or USB.
Choose Troubleshooting → Rescue a Red Hat Enterprise Linux system.
Once in the shell, we will identify partitions.
Step 2: List all disks and partitions

Use:

lsblk -f

What it does:

Lists all block devices, their mount points, filesystem types, and labels.

Example output:

NAME           FSTYPE      LABEL      MOUNTPOINT
sda
├─sda1         vfat        EFI       /boot/efi
├─sda2         xfs        boot       /boot
└─sda3         xfs                  /
sdb
└─sdb1         xfs                  /data

How to read it:

/ → root filesystem (sda3 in this example)
/boot → boot partition (sda2)
/boot/efi → EFI System Partition (sda1) for UEFI systems

On BIOS systems, you usually only have /boot and / (no /boot/efi).

Step 3: Mount the root filesystem
mkdir /mnt/sysimage
mount /dev/sda3 /mnt/sysimage

Explanation:

/dev/sda3 → root partition from lsblk.
Mounting it under /mnt/sysimage lets us chroot into it.

Effect:

Your installed Linux system files are now accessible at /mnt/sysimage.
Step 4: Mount the boot partition
BIOS system (legacy)
mount /dev/sda2 /mnt/sysimage/boot

Explanation:

/dev/sda2 → /boot partition.
Needed because GRUB2 stores its core files there.

Effect:

The bootloader files can now be recreated in /boot.
UEFI system
mount /dev/sda1 /mnt/sysimage/boot/efi

Explanation:

/dev/sda1 → EFI System Partition (FAT32).
Required for UEFI firmware to find GRUB2.

Effect:

GRUB2 can be installed into EFI and the system will boot via UEFI.
Step 5: Optional: Verify mounts
ls /mnt/sysimage

You should see:

bin, etc, home, var → root filesystem
boot (or boot/efi) → partition mounted correctly
Step 6: Summary of Example Mappings
System Type	Device	Mount Point in Rescue	Notes
BIOS	/dev/sda3	/mnt/sysimage	root /
BIOS	/dev/sda2	/mnt/sysimage/boot	bootloader files
UEFI	/dev/sda3	/mnt/sysimage	root /
UEFI	/dev/sda2	/mnt/sysimage/boot	kernel/initramfs files
UEFI	/dev/sda1	/mnt/sysimage/boot/efi	EFI System Partition, GRUB2 binaries

