Step 1: Know the IPs and subnet masks
Example:
IP1: 192.168.1.10
IP2: 192.168.1.20
Subnet mask for both: 255.255.255.0
Note: If the subnet masks differ, use each IP’s mask when calculating its network address.

Step 2: Convert the IP and subnet mask to binary
192.168.1.10 → 11000000.10101000.00000001.00001010
192.168.1.20 → 11000000.10101000.00000001.00010100
255.255.255.0 → 11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000

Step 3: Perform a bitwise AND between IP and subnet mask
IP1 AND mask:
11000000.10101000.00000001.00001010
AND
11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000
= 11000000.10101000.00000001.00000000 → 192.168.1.0
IP2 AND mask:
11000000.10101000.00000001.00010100
AND
11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000
= 11000000.10101000.00000001.00000000 → 192.168.1.0

Step 4: Compare the network addresses
Network address of IP1 = 192.168.1.0
Network address of IP2 = 192.168.1.0
✅ Same network → same subnet

Step 5: Quick shortcut (decimal method)
If subnet mask is 255.255.255.0, just compare the first three octets:
192.168.1.10 → 192.168.1
192.168.1.20 → 192.168.1
Same first three octets → same subnet

Step 1: Understand the subnet mask
Subnet mask: 255.255.254.0
In binary:
255 → 11111111
255 → 11111111
254 → 11111110
0   → 00000000
So /23 (23 bits for the network, 9 bits for hosts)
Each subnet spans 512 IP addresses (2^9), so the third octet may “combine” two numbers.

Step 2: Pick two example IPs
IP1: 192.168.0.10
IP2: 192.168.1.20
Subnet mask: 255.255.254.0

Step 3: Find network address
Bitwise AND between IP and mask.

IP1: 192.168.0.10
192 → 11000000
168 → 10101000
0   → 00000000
10  → 00001010
Mask: 255.255.254.0
255 → 11111111
255 → 11111111
254 → 11111110
0   → 00000000
Bitwise AND:
11000000.10101000.00000000.00001010
AND
11111111.11111111.11111110.00000000
=
11000000.10101000.00000000.00000000
→ Network address = 192.168.0.0

IP2: 192.168.1.20
192 → 11000000
168 → 10101000
1   → 00000001
20  → 00010100
Bitwise AND with mask:
11000000.10101000.00000001.00010100
AND
11111111.11111111.11111110.00000000
=
11000000.10101000.00000000.00000000
→ Network address = 192.168.0.0

✅ Both have the same network → same subnet

1️⃣ Network Address
The network address identifies the subnet a device belongs to.
Formula: Network Address = IP Address AND Subnet Mask
AND = bitwise AND: only 1 AND 1 = 1, everything else = 0

Example:
IP: 192.168.1.10
Subnet mask: 255.255.255.0

Step 1: Convert to binary
IP:   192.168.1.10 → 11000000.10101000.00000001.00001010
Mask: 255.255.255.0 → 11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000

Step 2: Bitwise AND
11000000.10101000.00000001.00001010
AND
11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000
=
11000000.10101000.00000001.00000000

Step 3: Convert back to decimal
11000000.10101000.00000001.00000000 → 192.168.1.0
✅ Network address = 192.168.1.0

2️⃣ Broadcast Address
The broadcast address is the last IP in the subnet—used to send messages to all devices in that subnet.
Formula: Broadcast Address = Network Address OR (~Subnet Mask)
~Subnet Mask = invert all bits
OR = bitwise OR: 1 OR 0 = 1

Step 1: Invert subnet mask
Subnet mask: 255.255.255.0 → 11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000
Inverted (~mask): 0.0.0.255 → 00000000.00000000.00000000.11111111

Step 2: OR with network address
Network: 192.168.1.0 → 11000000.10101000.00000001.00000000
OR
Inverted: 00000000.00000000.00000000.11111111
=
11000000.10101000.00000001.11111111

Step 3: Convert back to decimal
11000000.10101000.00000001.11111111 → 192.168.1.255
✅ Broadcast address = 192.168.1.255

1️⃣ Start with the subnet mask
/22 subnet mask = 255.255.252.0
Binary of third octet (first two are full 255):
252 → 11111100
Network bits in third octet = first 6 bits (1s)
Host bits in third octet = last 2 bits (0s)
Host bits in changing octet determine the subnet block size

2️⃣ Calculate the block size
Block Size = 256 − Subnet Mask value in that octet
Third octet mask = 252
256 − 252 = 4
Block size = 4 → amount the network address increments for each subnet in that octet

3️⃣ How this leads to multiples
First subnet starts at 0
Next subnet = 0 + block size = 4
Next = 4 + block size = 8
Next = 8 + block size = 12
And so on…
✅ /22 subnets in third octet increment in multiples of 4: 0, 4, 8, 12, …

4️⃣ Example applied to 192.168.5.10
Third octet = 5
Block size = 4 → multiples of 4: 0, 4, 8, …
Largest multiple ≤ 5 → 4
Network address = 192.168.4.0
Broadcast = last IP in that block → 192.168.7.255

✅ Shortcut Summary
Look at subnet mask → find octet where subnet bits end / host bits start
Block size = 256 − mask value in that octet
Network addresses = multiples of block size in that octet
For any IP, nearest multiple ≤ IP’s value = network address
Broadcast = network + block size − 1

1️⃣ Recall the network address
IP = 192.168.5.10
/22 subnet → block size = 4
Network address = 192.168.4.0
This block covers third octet values 4, 5, 6, 7

2️⃣ Understand the broadcast address
Broadcast = last IP in the subnet block
For /22, block spans 4 third-octet values because block size = 4

3️⃣ Shortcut to calculate broadcast address
Network third octet = 4
Add (block size − 1) → 4 + (4 − 1) = 7
Set fourth octet to 255
✅ Broadcast address = 192.168.7.255

4️⃣ Usable IPs
First usable = network + 1 → 192.168.4.1
Last usable = broadcast − 1 → 192.168.7.254
All devices in this subnet use IPs 192.168.4.1 → 192.168.7.254

5️⃣ Quick mental picture
Network = start of block → 192.168.4.0
Broadcast = end of block → 192.168.7.255
Block size = third octet increment = 4 → spans 4,5,6,7
Shortcut formula: broadcast = network third octet + block size − 1, fourth octet = 255

Step 1: Know your IP and subnet mask
IP = 192.168.5.10
Subnet mask = /20 → 255.255.240.0

Step 2: Determine which octet changes
Subnet mask in binary: 255.255.240.0 → 11111111.11111111.11110000.00000000
Network bits = 20, Host bits = 12
Changing octet = third octet (because first two octets are fully 255)

Block size formula:
Block size = 256 − value of subnet mask in changing octet
Third octet mask = 240 → 256 − 240 = 16
✅ Network addresses increment in multiples of 16: 0, 16, 32, 48, … 240

Step 3: Find the network address
IP’s third octet = 5
Nearest multiple of 16 ≤ 5 → 0
✅ Network address = 192.168.0.0
Subnet spans third octet 0 → 15

Step 4: Find the broadcast address
Last third octet = network third octet + block size − 1 → 0 + 16 − 1 = 15
Fourth octet = 255 (all host bits set to 1)
✅ Broadcast address = 192.168.15.255

Step 5: Determine usable IP range
First usable = network + 1 → 192.168.0.1
Last usable = broadcast − 1 → 192.168.15.254

