ip link show

1: lo: <LOOPBACK,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 65536 qdisc noqueue state UNKNOWN mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000
    link/loopback 00:00:00:00:00:00 brd 00:00:00:00:00:00
2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500 qdisc fq_codel state UP mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000
    link/ether 08:00:27:6d:8c:5e brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
3: enp0s8: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> mtu 1500 qdisc noop state DOWN mode DEFAULT group default qlen 1000
    link/ether 08:00:27:1e:2b:45 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff

Interface Index and Name

1: lo:
2: enp0s3:

- 1 and 2 are interface indices (internal to the kernel)
- lo is the loopback interface
- enp0s3 is a physical (or virtual) network interface

Flags <...>
Common flags:
- UP → Interface is enabled
- LOWER_UP → Physical link is active
- BROADCAST → Supports broadcast
- MULTICAST → Supports multicast traffic
- LOOPBACK → Loopback interface

MTU – Maximum Transmission Unit (packet size limit)
Example: mtu 1500

qdisc – Queueing discipline (how packets are scheduled)
Example: qdisc fq_codel

state – Interface state
Common states: UP, DOWN, UNKNOWN

MAC Address / Link Type

link/ether 08:00:27:6d:8c:5e brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
- link/ether → Ethernet interface
- 08:00:27:6d:8c:5e → MAC address
- brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff → Broadcast address

Using `ip link show`

Look at the flags and state fields:

- LOWER_UP → The physical layer is active (cable plugged in, switch port connected)  
- Absence of LOWER_UP or NO-CARRIER → No physical connection

Examples:

1. 2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> ... state UP  
   - LOWER_UP → Interface is physically connected  
   - UP → Interface is administratively enabled

2. 3: enp0s8: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP> ... state DOWN  
   - No LOWER_UP → Cable not connected or link down

Step 0: Preparation
Before you touch configuration files:
Know your network details
IPv4: IP address, netmask, gateway, DNS
IPv6: IP address/prefix, gateway, DNS
Interface name (find it with ip link show)
Ensure you have root privileges
Most network changes require sudo or root access.
Backup existing configs
sudo cp /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3 /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3.bak
Step 1: Identify the interface
ip link show
Example output:
2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> ...
Here, interface name is enp0s3.
Step 2: Edit the interface file
RHEL stores interface configs in:
/etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-<interface_name>
sudo vi /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3
Step 2a: Static IPv4 configuration
Example:
DEVICE=enp0s3
BOOTPROTO=static
ONBOOT=yes
IPADDR=192.168.1.100
NETMASK=255.255.255.0
GATEWAY=192.168.1.1
DNS1=8.8.8.8
DNS2=8.8.4.4
Explanation:
BOOTPROTO=static → manual IP
ONBOOT=yes → activate at boot
IPADDR & NETMASK → your IPv4
GATEWAY → default route
DNS1 / DNS2 → resolvers
Step 2b: Static IPv6 configuration
Add these lines in the same file:
IPV6INIT=yes
IPV6ADDR=2001:db8:1::100/64
IPV6_DEFAULTGW=2001:db8:1::1
DNS1=2001:4860:4860::8888
DNS2=2001:4860:4860::8844
Explanation:
IPV6INIT=yes → enable IPv6
IPV6ADDR → static IPv6 with prefix length
IPV6_DEFAULTGW → IPv6 gateway
DNS1 / DNS2 → IPv6 DNS servers
⚠️ Make sure IPv6 addresses don’t conflict with existing devices.
Step 3: Enable and restart networking
sudo nmcli connection reload
sudo nmcli connection up enp0s3
Or using the old network service (RHEL 7+ uses NetworkManager by default):
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
Step 4: Verify the configuration
Check IP addresses:
ip addr show enp0s3
Check connectivity:
ping 192.168.1.1      # IPv4 gateway
ping6 2001:db8:1::1   # IPv6 gateway
Check DNS resolution:
ping google.com
ping6 google.com
Check default routes:
ip route show
ip -6 route show
Step 5: Persist and finalize
Configuration is already saved in /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3.
Ensure firewall rules allow necessary traffic.
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ssh
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Document IP assignments for future reference.

1️⃣ Make sure your DHCP server is running
Check the DHCP service on your server:
sudo systemctl status dhcpd
Status should be active (running).
The server config is usually in:
/etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf
Make sure the subnet your client is on is included in the DHCP server config:
subnet 192.168.1.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
    range 192.168.1.100 192.168.1.200;
    option routers 192.168.1.1;
    option domain-name-servers 8.8.8.8, 8.8.4.4;
}
2️⃣ Configure the client to use DHCP
On the client RHEL machine:
Option A: Using nmcli (NetworkManager CLI)
Set IPv4 to DHCP:
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv4.method auto
Optionally, enable DHCP for IPv6:
sudo nmcli connection modify enp0s3 ipv6.method auto
Bring the interface up:
sudo nmcli connection up enp0s3
Option B: Using interface config file
Edit the interface file:
sudo vi /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-enp0s3
Make sure it has:
DEVICE=enp0s3
BOOTPROTO=dhcp
ONBOOT=yes
Restart NetworkManager:
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
3️⃣ Verify DHCP IP
Check which IP the client got from your DHCP server:
ip addr show enp0s3
You should see an IP in the range configured on the DHCP server, e.g., 192.168.1.100–200.
Check the lease:
sudo cat /var/lib/NetworkManager/dhclient-*.lease
4️⃣ Test Connectivity
Ping the default gateway (router):
ping -c 3 192.168.1.1
Ping the DHCP server itself:
ping -c 3 <DHCP-server-IP>
Ping an external IP to test Internet access (if allowed):
ping -c 3 8.8.8.8
5️⃣ What Happens Behind the Scenes
The client broadcasts a DHCPDISCOVER message.
The DHCP server responds with a DHCPOFFER including IP, subnet mask, gateway, and DNS.
The client requests that IP using DHCPREQUEST.
The server confirms with DHCPACK.
The client configures its interface with the IP and other settings.

Identify your interface
First, find your network interface:
ip link show
Example output:
1: lo: ...
2: enp0s3: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500 ...
Let’s assume your interface is enp0s3.
2️⃣ Decide your static IP and network details
You need:
Parameter	Example Value	Notes
IP Address	192.168.1.50	Must be unique in your LAN
Subnet Mask	255.255.255.0	Usually /24 for small networks
Gateway	192.168.1.1	Router or internet gateway
DNS	8.8.8.8 (Google)	Needed for domain name resolution
